DETAIL = "\n      <section class=\"model-reference model-reference-deep shell\" id=\"rosalind-pricing\" aria-labelledby=\"rosalind-pricing-heading\">\n        <div class=\"model-section-head\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">API ECONOMICS</p><h2 id=\"rosalind-pricing-heading\">GPT-Rosalind Research pricing: input, cache and output tokens.</h2></div>\n        <p>OpenAI's life-sciences rate card prices <code>gpt-rosalind-research</code> by separately metered input, cached input and generated output tokens. The schedule began <strong>October 5, 2026</strong>. The model has <strong>no separate cache-write price</strong> on the published rate card. Actual charges can differ when a request uses billable tools or special deployment arrangements.</p>\n        <div style=\"overflow-x:auto\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Billing component</th><th scope=\"col\">USD per 1 million tokens</th><th scope=\"col\">When it applies</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n          <tr><th scope=\"row\">New input tokens</th><td>$5.00</td><td>Input not billed at cached-input rate</td></tr>\n          <tr><th scope=\"row\">Cached input tokens</th><td>$0.50</td><td>Eligible previously cached input, when caching applies</td></tr>\n          <tr><th scope=\"row\">Generated output tokens</th><td>$25.00</td><td>Tokens generated by the model</td></tr>\n        </tbody></table></div>\n        <div class=\"model-caveat-list\">\n          <div><span>01</span><p><strong>Example without caching:</strong> 100,000 new input tokens and 10,000 output tokens cost about <strong>$0.75</strong> ($0.50 input + $0.25 output).</p></div>\n          <div><span>02</span><p><strong>Example with caching:</strong> 20,000 new input tokens, 80,000 eligible cached input tokens and 5,000 output tokens cost about <strong>$0.265</strong> ($0.10 + $0.04 + $0.125). Cached-input discounts only apply to tokens actually billed as cached.</p></div>\n          <div><span>03</span><p><strong>Cost caveat:</strong> the amount of biological evidence retrieved, repeated iterations, scientific tools, hosting or regulated-workspace conditions can affect the true workflow cost. These examples are token-only estimates, not enterprise quotations.</p></div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"model-related-links\"><a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><span>OFFICIAL RATE CARD</span><strong>OpenAI API pricing</strong><b>↗</b></a><a href=\"/models/pricing/\"><span>MODEL ECONOMICS</span><strong>SXF token pricing comparison</strong><b>↗</b></a><a href=\"/tools/ai-model-cost-calculator/\"><span>CALCULATOR</span><strong>Estimate token costs</strong><b>↗</b></a></div>\n      </section>\n      <section class=\"model-reference model-reference-deep shell\" id=\"rosalind-capabilities\" aria-labelledby=\"rosalind-capabilities-heading\">\n        <div class=\"model-section-head\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">LIFE SCIENCES CAPABILITIES</p><h2 id=\"rosalind-capabilities-heading\">What GPT-Rosalind can help researchers investigate.</h2></div>\n        <p>OpenAI describes the GPT-Rosalind series as purpose-built for scientific reasoning and tool-connected workflows, rather than general chat alone. The most defensible way to assess its value is to examine scientific tasks, source traceability, tool results and expert validation—not assume every capability shown in a Rosalind Workbench demo is a native API feature of <code>gpt-rosalind-research</code>.</p>\n        <div class=\"model-family-grid\">\n          <article class=\"model-family-card\"><span>EVIDENCE SYNTHESIS</span><h3>Literature and hypotheses</h3><p>Compare papers, experimental findings and competing mechanisms; trace conclusions to cited evidence and identify uncertainties requiring validation.</p></article>\n          <article class=\"model-family-card\"><span>GENOMICS</span><h3>Sequencing and quantitative biology</h3><p>Support reasoning around RNA-seq, single-cell workflows, variants, quality-control decisions and interpretation of supplied analytical results.</p></article>\n          <article class=\"model-family-card\"><span>DRUG DISCOVERY</span><h3>Medicinal chemistry</h3><p>Evaluate structure–activity relationships, lead-optimization trade-offs and candidate evidence for review by qualified chemistry and discovery teams.</p></article>\n          <article class=\"model-family-card\"><span>LAB OPERATIONS</span><h3>Experimental planning</h3><p>Organize literature, troubleshooting evidence and potential next experiments; require controlled tools and human verification of resulting plans.</p></article>\n        </div>\n        <p><strong>Modality distinction:</strong> SXF's current canonical record specifies <strong>text input and text output</strong> for this API model. OpenAI's broader Rosalind ecosystem can work with structures, sequences, images and scientific datasets through scientific tools and visualizers; that is not evidence of direct, unrestricted multimodal API support for this model ID. Context size, max output and knowledge cutoff also remain <strong>not publicly specified</strong> for this exact API model.</p>\n        <p>For research workflow design, see <a href=\"/guides/model-context-protocol-mcp/\">how AI agents connect to scientific tools through MCP</a> and the <a href=\"/guides/ai-agent-evaluation-checklist/\">agent evaluation checklist</a>.</p>\n      </section>\n      <section class=\"model-reference model-reference-deep shell\" id=\"rosalind-evaluations\" aria-labelledby=\"rosalind-evaluation-heading\">\n        <div class=\"model-section-head\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">PUBLISHED EVIDENCE / BENCHMARK CONTEXT</p><h2 id=\"rosalind-evaluation-heading\">What OpenAI's Rosalind evaluations actually show.</h2></div>\n        <p>OpenAI's <a href=\"https://openai.com/index/introducing-new-capabilities-to-gpt-rosalind/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">June 3 scientific update</a> reports improvements over GPT-5.5 on specialist tests. These are <strong>OpenAI-reported GPT-Rosalind-series results</strong>, not independent third-party proof for every deployment or an explicitly isolated score for the <code>gpt-rosalind-research</code> SKU. Treat them as direction for evaluation, not guaranteed outcomes.</p>\n        <div style=\"overflow-x:auto\"><table class=\"data-table\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">OpenAI-reported evaluation</th><th scope=\"col\">GPT-Rosalind series</th><th scope=\"col\">GPT-5.5</th><th scope=\"col\">Additional context</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n          <tr><th scope=\"row\">MedChemBench</th><td>27.5%</td><td>25.1%</td><td>7.2% fewer tokens reported</td></tr>\n          <tr><th scope=\"row\">GeneBench</th><td>21.6%</td><td>20.4%</td><td>31% fewer tokens reported</td></tr>\n          <tr><th scope=\"row\">LabWorkBench</th><td>63.2%</td><td>55.8%</td><td>5.3% fewer tokens reported</td></tr>\n        </tbody></table></div>\n        <p>These tests measure different tasks: medicinal chemistry questions, end-to-end quantitative genomics work and wet-lab protocol assistance. Do not average the percentages or interpret them as clinical success rates. A practical buyer's evaluation should sample the organization's own assays, domain evidence, quality standards, tool permissions and reproducibility requirements.</p>\n      </section>\n      <section class=\"model-reference model-reference-deep shell\" id=\"rosalind-access\" aria-labelledby=\"rosalind-access-heading\">\n        <div class=\"model-section-head\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">ENTERPRISE ACCESS AND GOVERNANCE</p><h2 id=\"rosalind-access-heading\">How to access GPT-Rosalind Research and where it fits.</h2></div>\n        <div class=\"model-caveat-list\">\n          <div><span>01</span><p><strong>Request approval.</strong> OpenAI lists GPT-Rosalind as available to eligible organizations globally through a <strong>trusted-access</strong> process. The specific API use is approved internal research; a paid OpenAI API account alone does not guarantee model access.</p></div>\n          <div><span>02</span><p><strong>Verify your product surface.</strong> The family is discussed across enterprise ChatGPT, Codex/Workbench and the API. Confirm that the approved organization has permission for the API model ID, not simply access to a general research workspace.</p></div>\n          <div><span>03</span><p><strong>Control tools and data.</strong> Validate scientific source provenance, role-based access, retention and sensitive-data handling, tool write permissions, audit trails and independent review before integrating internal datasets or automating experiments.</p></div>\n          <div><span>04</span><p><strong>Keep research and clinical decisions distinct.</strong> Generated hypotheses, analyses and recommended follow-ups must be checked against evidence and domain expertise. Model availability does not imply regulatory clearance or clinically validated outputs.</p></div>\n        </div>\n        <p><strong>Rosalind Research vs Workbench:</strong> the model is an approved API capability; <strong>Rosalind Workbench</strong> is a dedicated research workspace with tool-connected workflows such as sequencing quality control. OpenAI's Help Center states that researchers can use Workbench without gaining separate access to the GPT-Rosalind model itself. The two should not be priced or evaluated as interchangeable products.</p>\n        <div class=\"model-related-links\"><a href=\"https://openai.com/gpt-rosalind/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><span>OFFICIAL ACCESS</span><strong>Rosalind eligibility and request</strong><b>↗</b></a><a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001193-gpt-rosalind-for-life-sciences-research\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><span>HELP CENTER</span><strong>Workbench, API and permission differences</strong><b>↗</b></a><a href=\"/providers/openai/\"><span>PROVIDER CATALOG</span><strong>OpenAI model directory</strong><b>↗</b></a></div>\n      </section>\n      <section class=\"model-reference model-reference-deep shell\" id=\"rosalind-api\" aria-labelledby=\"rosalind-api-heading\">\n        <div class=\"model-section-head\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">FOR APPROVED DEVELOPERS</p><h2 id=\"rosalind-api-heading\">GPT-Rosalind Research Responses API example.</h2></div>\n        <p>The <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/resources/responses/methods/create\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenAI Responses API model reference</a> includes <code>gpt-rosalind-research</code>. Only approved organizations should attempt the call below. Keep credentials server-side; validate enabled features and policy requirements, and inspect usage and content before relying on generated scientific interpretations.</p>\n        <pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-x:auto;overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><code>curl https://api.openai.com/v1/responses \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"model\": \"gpt-rosalind-research\",\n    \"input\": \"Summarize the evidence for and against a research hypothesis using the supplied cited literature. Identify uncertainty, confounders, and experiments that could falsify it.\"\n  }'</code></pre>\n        <p>This is an illustrative request shape, not a claim that an unapproved key will succeed. The model's published context capacity, maximum output and exact supported tool configurations should be confirmed in an approved environment; do not copy arbitrary tool parameters from unrelated models.</p>\n      </section>\n      <section class=\"model-reference model-reference-deep shell\" id=\"rosalind-questions\" aria-labelledby=\"rosalind-faq-heading\">\n        <div class=\"model-section-head\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">QUESTIONS FROM DEVELOPERS AND RESEARCHERS</p><h2 id=\"rosalind-faq-heading\">GPT-Rosalind Research FAQ.</h2></div>\n        <details><summary>What is GPT-Rosalind Research?</summary><p>GPT-Rosalind Research is OpenAI's specialized reasoning model for eligible life-sciences research organizations. Its API model ID is gpt-rosalind-research. It supports research reasoning and workflows involving scientific data and tools; access requires approval.</p></details>\n        <details><summary>How much does gpt-rosalind-research cost on the OpenAI API?</summary><p>OpenAI publishes $5.00 per million input tokens, $0.50 per million cached input tokens and $25.00 per million output tokens. Published billing began on October 5, 2026. These are base token rates, not an all-in quote for tools or infrastructure.</p></details>\n        <details><summary>Is GPT-Rosalind Research available to everyone?</summary><p>No. GPT-Rosalind is available globally to eligible organizations through OpenAI's trusted-access program. API access is limited to approved internal research, and organizations should verify approval for the specific product surface they intend to use.</p></details>\n        <details><summary>What is the context window for GPT-Rosalind Research?</summary><p>OpenAI's referenced public materials do not state a verified context-window size for the gpt-rosalind-research API model. SXF therefore labels it not published rather than inferring a token capacity from other GPT models.</p></details>\n        <details><summary>What is GPT-Rosalind Research's maximum output token limit?</summary><p>A verified maximum output token limit is not published in the sources reviewed for this exact model ID. Developers should check the latest approved API model documentation and account limits before setting production budgets.</p></details>\n        <details><summary>What is the difference between GPT-Rosalind Research and Rosalind Workbench?</summary><p>GPT-Rosalind Research is an API model ID. Rosalind Workbench is a separate research workspace that organizes scientific tools and workflows in ChatGPT and Codex; OpenAI states Workbench can be used without GPT-Rosalind model access.</p></details>\n        <details><summary>Which scientific tasks is GPT-Rosalind designed to support?</summary><p>OpenAI describes literature and evidence synthesis, target research and validation, genomics and sequencing interpretation, protein and sequence analysis, medicinal chemistry, wet-lab troubleshooting, and experiment planning. Model outputs still need independent scientific verification.</p></details>\n        <details><summary>Can GPT-Rosalind Research be used through the OpenAI Responses API?</summary><p>OpenAI includes gpt-rosalind-research among model identifiers accepted for Responses API creation. The organization must first be approved, and enabled tools, limits, and permissions should be checked for its deployment.</p></details>\n        <details><summary>How does GPT-Rosalind compare with general-purpose GPT models?</summary><p>OpenAI reports family-level life-sciences evaluation gains over GPT-5.5 on medicinal chemistry, genomics and lab-work tasks. Those scores come from OpenAI's disclosed evaluations and are not a guaranteed result or a verified independent benchmark for every API version.</p></details>\n        <details><summary>What are GPT-Rosalind's input and output modalities?</summary><p>SXF's verified model catalog lists text input and text output for the gpt-rosalind-research API model. Scientific image, sequence, and structure workflows may also involve connected tools or workspace viewers, which should not be mistaken for a separately documented direct API modality.</p></details>\n        <details><summary>Does GPT-Rosalind replace scientific experts or clinical validation?</summary><p>No. It is a research-assistance model for generating and checking scientific hypotheses and using approved tools. Experimental results, literature provenance, access permissions and expert review remain essential; outputs do not establish clinical safety, efficacy or regulatory approval.</p></details>\n        <details><summary>When was GPT-Rosalind Research introduced and made billable?</summary><p>OpenAI announced GPT-Rosalind on April 16, 2026, introduced expanded scientific capabilities on June 3, announced wider trusted-access eligibility on September 11, and started the published gpt-rosalind-research API billing schedule on October 5, 2026.</p></details>\n      </section>\n      <section class=\"model-reference model-reference-deep shell\" id=\"rosalind-primary-sources\" aria-labelledby=\"rosalind-sources-heading\">\n        <div class=\"model-section-head\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">EVIDENCE AND FRESHNESS</p><h2 id=\"rosalind-sources-heading\">Release timeline and official documentation.</h2></div>\n        <div class=\"model-caveat-list\">\n          <div><span>APR 16</span><p><strong>2026:</strong> OpenAI introduced the GPT-Rosalind model family for biology, drug discovery and translational research.</p></div>\n          <div><span>JUN 03</span><p><strong>2026:</strong> OpenAI published expanded science workflow capabilities and MedChemBench, GeneBench and LabWorkBench evaluation results.</p></div>\n          <div><span>SEP 11</span><p><strong>2026:</strong> OpenAI announced broader access for qualifying organizations through its trusted-access program.</p></div>\n          <div><span>OCT 05</span><p><strong>2026:</strong> the published <code>gpt-rosalind-research</code> API billing rates took effect.</p></div>\n        </div>\n        <p><strong>Source of truth:</strong> OpenAI's API pricing is the authority for rates; its Rosalind product materials and Help Center describe access and research workflows. Dates of the product announcements do not establish a separate release date for the API model ID. This page retains SXF's original model-history ledger and uses that ledger for tracked changes.</p>\n        <div class=\"model-related-links\"><a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenAI API pricing ↗</a><a href=\"https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-rosalind/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Original model-family announcement ↗</a><a href=\"https://openai.com/index/introducing-new-capabilities-to-gpt-rosalind/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Updated science capabilities ↗</a><a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001193-gpt-rosalind-for-life-sciences-research\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Enterprise access and Workbench ↗</a></div>\n      </section>\n"


def enrich_gpt_rosalind_research_page(page: str) -> str:
    if 'data-rosalind-research-enrichment' in page:
        return page
    if f'<link rel="canonical" href="{CANONICAL}"' not in page:
        raise ValueError("GPT-Rosalind Research canonical changed")
    old_title = 'GPT-Rosalind Research — API Specs, Context &amp; Pricing | SXF / AI'
    if page.count('<title>' + old_title + '</title>') != 1:
        raise ValueError("Rosalind model title template drift")
    page = page.replace('<title>' + old_title + '</title>', '<title>' + escape(TITLE) + '</title>', 1)
    for name, value in [
        ('description', DESCRIPTION), ('og:title', TITLE), ('og:description', DESCRIPTION),
        ('twitter:title', TITLE), ('twitter:description', DESCRIPTION),
    ]:
        pattern = r'(<meta (?:name|property)="' + re.escape(name) + r'" content=")[^"]*(" />)'
        page, count = re.subn(pattern, lambda m: m.group(1) + escape(value, quote=True) + m.group(2), page, count=1)
        if count != 1:
            raise ValueError('Rosalind missing meta: ' + name)

    old_h1 = '<h1>GPT-Rosalind Research<br><span>verified model reference.</span></h1>'
    if page.count(old_h1) != 1:
        raise ValueError('Rosalind H1 template drift')
    page = page.replace(old_h1, '<h1>GPT-Rosalind Research<br><span>API pricing, capabilities and access.</span></h1>', 1)
    old_lead = '<p>OpenAI specialized reasoning model for approved life-sciences research workflows and scientific tool use.</p>'
    if page.count(old_lead) != 1:
        raise ValueError('Rosalind hero lead template drift')
    page = page.replace(old_lead, '<p>' + escape(LEAD) + '</p>', 1)

    main_reference = '      <section class="model-reference model-reference-deep shell">'
    if page.count(main_reference) != 1:
        raise ValueError('Rosalind model reference insertion anchor changed')
    page = page.replace(main_reference, QUICK + '\n\n' + main_reference, 1)

    change_watch = '      <section class="model-change-watch shell"'
    if page.count(change_watch) != 1:
        raise ValueError('Rosalind history marker changed')
    page = page.replace(change_watch, DETAIL + '\n' + change_watch, 1)

    match = re.search(r'(<script type="application/ld\+json">)(.*?)(</script>)', page, re.DOTALL)
    if match is None:
        raise ValueError('Rosalind schema template drift')
    schema = json.loads(match.group(2))
    graph = schema['@graph']
    article = next(x for x in graph if x.get('@type') == 'TechArticle')
    crumb = next(x for x in graph if x.get('@type') == 'BreadcrumbList')
    article.update({
        'headline': H1, 'description': DESCRIPTION, 'dateModified': '2026-10-13',
        'mainEntityOfPage': {'@id': CANONICAL + '#webpage'},
        'isPartOf': {'@id': 'https://sxf.si/#website'},
        'publisher': {'@id': 'https://sxf.si/#organization'},
        'author': {'@id': 'https://sxf.si/#organization'},
        'citation': SOURCES,
        'about': [
            {'@type': 'Thing', 'name': 'GPT-Rosalind Research', 'identifier': 'gpt-rosalind-research'},
            {'@type': 'Organization', 'name': 'OpenAI', 'url': 'https://openai.com/'},
            {'@type': 'Thing', 'name': 'life sciences AI models'},
        ],
        'keywords': ['GPT-Rosalind Research', 'gpt-rosalind-research', 'GPT Rosalind API pricing', 'GPT Rosalind context window', 'OpenAI life sciences AI model', 'GPT Rosalind access', 'drug discovery AI'],
        'proficiencyLevel': 'Intermediate',
    })
    crumb['@id'] = CANONICAL + '#breadcrumb'
    graph.append({
        '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': CANONICAL + '#webpage',
        'url': CANONICAL, 'name': TITLE, 'description': DESCRIPTION,
        'isPartOf': {'@id': 'https://sxf.si/#website'}, 'mainEntity': {'@id': CANONICAL + '#article'},
        'breadcrumb': {'@id': CANONICAL + '#breadcrumb'}, 'inLanguage': 'en',
    })
    graph.append({
        '@type': 'FAQPage', '@id': CANONICAL + '#faq',
        'url': CANONICAL + '#rosalind-questions',
        'mainEntity': [{'@type':'Question','name':q,'acceptedAnswer': {'@type':'Answer','text':a}} for q,a in FAQS],
    })
    graph.append({'@type':'WebSite','@id':'https://sxf.si/#website','name':'SXF / AI','url':'https://sxf.si/'})
    graph.append({'@type':'Organization','@id':'https://sxf.si/#organization','name':'SXF / AI','url':'https://sxf.si/'})
    encoded = json.dumps(schema, ensure_ascii=False, separators=(',', ':')).replace('<', '\\u003c')
    page = page[:match.start(2)] + encoded + page[match.end(2):]
    return page
